\subsection{Activation hint for a fresh venv}
\label{sec:setup-activation-hint}

When [[init_python]] creates a venv, the student still has to
activate it before running [[python]] or [[pytest]] in the
same shell---[[init]] cannot do that for them, because a
child process cannot mutate its parent shell's environment.
A one-line hint printed after creation is therefore the best
we can do.

The hint differs by platform because the activation scripts
do: POSIX shells expect [[source .venv/bin/activate]],
whereas Windows venvs place the scripts in [[Scripts\]] and
have no [[source]] builtin (the student types the path to the
activator directly, and both [[cmd.exe]] and PowerShell pick
up the right extension via [[PATHEXT]]).  This is the same
POSIX/Windows layout split that [[find_in_venv]] already
branches on in \cref{sec:setup-pip-in-venv}, so factoring it
into its own helper keeps the two concerns in sync---if
Windows ever drops [[Scripts\]], both helpers fail together
and the fix is localised.

We branch on [[sys.platform]] rather than [[os.name]]
because [[pathlib]] inspects [[os.name]] at [[Path]]
construction time to pick POSIX vs. Windows semantics.
A test that monkeypatched [[os.name]] to [[nt]] would
silently switch every [[Path]] created afterwards---including
paths pytest builds internally for its own error
reporting---to Windows flavour, and the first backslash in a
rendered path triggers a [[ValueError]] deep inside pytest's
terminal writer.  [[sys.platform]] has no such side effect.

<<helper functions>>=
def venv_activation_hint(venv_dir):
    """Return a shell-appropriate activation hint for ``venv_dir``.

    Used by :func:`init_python` to nudge the student after a
    fresh venv is created.  POSIX shells need ``source
    .../bin/activate``; Windows shells run
    ``...\\Scripts\\activate`` directly.
    """
    if sys.platform.startswith("win"):
        return f"Next step: {venv_dir}\\Scripts\\activate"
    return f"Next step: source {venv_dir}/bin/activate"
@

