\subsection{The [[tutorial]] command}
\label{sec:tutorial}

The tutorial subcommand delegates to [[pytorial]] rather than
reimplementing a second step engine inside [[learnlog]].
That keeps the host CLI focused on learnlog's own behavior while
still letting us ship guided walkthroughs for common tasks such as
project setup, playback, sharing, and ProgSnap2 analysis.

We mount pytorial's embedded command tree under [[learnlog
tutorial]], which gives us [[list]], [[run]], and [[review]]
without the standalone [[install]] command that only makes sense
for pytorial's top-level CLI.
Passing [[namespace="learnlog"]] keeps pytorial's persisted state
isolated under a learnlog-specific directory instead of mixing it
with other embedded hosts on the same machine.
The builtin [[using-tutorials]] self-tour is prepended
automatically, and the rest of the catalog comes from the Markdown
tutorials we ship as package data under [[learnlog/tutorials/]].
We list those tutorial files explicitly so [[learnlog tutorial
list]] follows the intended student-to-researcher progression
instead of an incidental alphabetical order.

<<typer commands>>=
_builtin_tutorials_dir = resources.files("learnlog") / "tutorials"

pytorial.add_typer_subcommand(
    app,
    name="tutorial",
    namespace="learnlog",
    tutorial_paths=(
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "getting-started.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "playback-and-tagging.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "export-and-share.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "analysing-progsnap2.md"),
    ),
)
@

Like [[play]] in \cref{sec:play}, the interactive execution path
needs a real terminal, so our CLI regression test stays on the
TTY-free [[list]] subcommand.
That is enough to verify the host-side wiring and that the shipped
tutorial resources are visible to pytorial.

The getting-started tutorial should still hand the learner over to a
normal shell step for the first file creation task, so the student opens
[[hello.py]] in a preferred editor from the terminal.
With pytorial's shell hooks available for integrated tutorials, every
shell step after the explicit activation lesson installs
[[eval "$(learnlog activate)"]] as a [[pre_command]].
That keeps the tutorial honest about fresh shells while still making the
project environment available for any extra commands the student tries
before returning from the step.
The run step requires a [[python hello.py]] invocation and a greeting on
stdout.
We express both with [[pytorial]] regex matches rather than plain
[[contains]] strings so that equally valid solutions are not rejected:
the command tolerates [[python3]] and a leading [[./]], and the greeting
tolerates case and punctuation variation around [[hello]] and [[world]].
The final playback step looks for [[learnlog play -b]] (accepting the
long [[--batch]] alternative through a regex), then verifies the
recorded run by matching [[hello.py]] and the same lenient greeting,
plus the stable [[diff --git]] header that proves a real diff was
rendered.
We deliberately drop the [[Script: hello.py]] and
[[stdout: Hello, world!]] line prefixes from the validation: those
strings are an internal output format of [[learnlog play -b]], not
anything the student types, and pinning the tutorial to that exact
format would make it fragile to future viewer changes without
strengthening the learning signal.
We lock the resulting mixed-mode pattern list in with regression tests
against the generated tutorial resource.

