\subsection{The [[init]] command}
\label{sec:init}

The command itself is a thin wrapper over the setup
pipeline from \cref{sec:setup}.  It anchors the repository
at the chosen directory by constructing a [[LearnlogRepo]]
(whose constructor is idempotent---see \cref{gitrepo}),
then dispatches to the language-specific installer.  The
language argument is typed as [[Language]] so Typer
rejects unknown toolchains before we are ever called.

The optional [[-C]] / [[--dir]] flag mirrors the pattern
used by [[set-remote]] and [[push]] and makes [[init]]
scriptable from a parent directory.  The [[--python]]
option is the [[init]]-specific escape hatch discussed in
\cref{sec:setup-target-python}.

<<typer commands>>=
@app.command(name="init")
def init(
    language: Annotated[
        Language,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Language toolchain to set up.",
        ),
    ],
    directory: Annotated[
        str | None,
        typer.Option(
            "-C", "--dir",
            help=(
                "Project directory to set up. "
                "Defaults to the current directory."
            ),
        ),
    ] = None,
    python: Annotated[
        str | None,
        typer.Option(
            "--python",
            help=(
                "Target Python interpreter (name on "
                "PATH or full path).  Defaults to "
                "python3."
            ),
        ),
    ] = None,
):
    """Set up a project to record with learnlog."""
    workdir = (
        pathlib.Path(directory).resolve()
        if directory
        else pathlib.Path.cwd()
    )
    LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    if language is Language.python:
        init_python(workdir, python)
