\subsection{The [[list]] command}
\label{sec:list-command}

The [[list]] command gives a compact overview of the development
log, one line per commit---like [[git log --oneline]] but showing
the start time and command instead of the commit message subject.
This is useful for quickly finding a session to inspect with
[[play]], or for checking what a student has been working on.

For symmetry with [[play]] and [[analyse]], the source is passed
with [[--from]] / [[-f]].  This keeps the positional interface free
for command-specific arguments and makes the source selection rule the
same across the commands that can read directories, bundles, or
ProgSnap2 exports.

When a commit contains multiple runs (because the student ran the
same code several times without editing), we show the first run's
details and append [[(N runs)]] so the teacher knows there is more
to explore.

The [[--success]] and [[--failure]] flags narrow the listing to
commits that contain at least one run of the requested outcome.
A teacher chasing down where a student got stuck passes
[[--failure]]; a student searching for a working version to
revert to passes [[--success]] and uses the last line as the
hash to check out.  Because a commit's outcomes form a set
(\cref{sec:commit-outcomes}), a commit with both a successful
and a failed run appears under both flags---this matters for the
recovery workflow, where the student's most recent known-good
commit must not be hidden just because they later re-ran the
code and broke it.

<<typer commands>>=
@app.command(name="list")
def list_commits(
    source: Annotated[
        str | None,
        typer.Option(
            "--from", "-f",
            help=(
                "Source: directory with .learnlog/, "
                "a ProgSnap2 zip, or a .learnlog bundle "
                "file. Defaults to current directory."
            ),
        ),
    ] = None,
    show_all: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--all", "-a",
            help=(
                "Include learnlog's own runs in the "
                "output."
            ),
        ),
    ] = False,
    success: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--success",
            help=(
                "Show only commits with at least "
                "one successful run."
            ),
        ),
    ] = False,
    failure: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--failure",
            help=(
                "Show only commits with at least "
                "one failed run."
            ),
        ),
    ] = False,
):
    """List commits with start time and command."""
    import shutil

    repo, tmpdir = resolve_source(source)
    try:
        commits = get_commits(repo)
        if not commits:
            raise SystemExit("No commits found.")

        filter_lr = not show_all
        trailers = get_run_trailers(repo)
        <<build wanted outcome set>>
        for commit in commits:
            <<get and summarise commit>>
    finally:
        if tmpdir is not None:
            shutil.rmtree(tmpdir, ignore_errors=True)
@

The two outcome flags share a single representation: a set of
outcome labels.  We build the set once before the commit loop
rather than branching on the flags inside it, and we rely on an
empty set meaning \enquote{no filter active} so the per-commit
check can short-circuit cheaply.

<<build wanted outcome set>>=
wanted = set()
if success:
    wanted.add("success")
if failure:
    wanted.add("failure")
@

For each commit we fetch the full message body, merge in the
trailers of the runs it records (\cref{sec:run-join}), optionally
filter out learnlog's own runs using [[filter_learnlog_runs]],
drop commits that do not match the outcome filter, and then
extract the summary with [[parse_run_summary]].
Commits whose runs are all filtered out (or that have no run
blocks, like the initial commit) are silently skipped.

Merging matters here beyond cosmetics: the outcome filters read the
exit code, which lives in the trailer.
Without the merge, [[--success]] and [[--failure]] would match
nothing at all, since every commit would look like an interrupted
run.

<<get and summarise commit>>=
body = repo.git(
    "log", "-1", "--format=%B", commit["hash"],
).stdout.rstrip("\n")
body = merge_run_trailers(body, trailers)
if filter_lr:
    body = filter_learnlog_runs(body)
<<skip commits that do not match the outcome filter>>
summary = parse_run_summary(body)
if summary is None:
    continue
command, start_time, run_count = summary
short_hash = commit["hash"][:7]
suffix = (
    f" ({run_count} runs)" if run_count > 1 else ""
)
typer.echo(
    f"{short_hash} {start_time} {command}{suffix}"
)
@

When at least one outcome flag is active (so [[wanted]] is
non-empty), we intersect it with the commit's outcomes
(\cref{sec:commit-outcomes}) and skip the commit if the
intersection is empty.  Set intersection gives exactly the
semantics we want: each flag contributes one outcome to
[[wanted]], passing both flags matches any commit with at least
one finalized run, and the [[unknown]] outcome from unfinalized
runs never matches because we never add it to [[wanted]].

<<skip commits that do not match the outcome filter>>=
if wanted and not (commit_outcomes(body) & wanted):
    continue
@

Let's verify that the command lists commits with the expected
format:

